The club itself was made of large osteoderms fused into a lump at the tip of the tail. The last part of the backbone before it was locked stiff by [[tendon|tendons]] that had turned to bone. That gave the club a rigid handle, a bit like the handle of a hammer or a [[@cricket-bat|cricket bat]].

The front of the tail stayed flexible, with strong [[@muscles|muscles]] near the hips. So the animal could swing the whole stiff end sideways in a wide arc. In 2009, a scientist used measurements of fossil clubs to estimate how hard they could hit. Large clubs, she found, could probably break bone. Smaller ones would only bruise.

{{story:knights-castles}}

Mars once had rivers, lakes and perhaps a shallow sea. Its surface is cold and dry today, but [[@first-life|microbes]] could have lived there long ago. NASA's [[@mars-rovers|Mars rovers]] are searching. In 2024, the [[rover|rover]] Perseverance found a rock with small speckled patterns that, on Earth, are often linked to microbes. Scientists say non-living chemistry could also explain them, and they hope one day to bring the sample home to check.

[[@europa|Europa]], a moon of Jupiter, and [[@space-weird-oceans-moons|Enceladus]], a moon of [[@saturn-rings|Saturn]], both have oceans of liquid water under their ice. Enceladus sprays some of its ocean into space, so a spacecraft can sample it without drilling. Saturn's largest moon, [[@titan|Titan]], has lakes of liquid methane and a thick, hazy [[atmosphere|atmosphere]]. Any life there would have to be very strange. NASA's Dragonfly, a flying drone, is due to launch there later this decade.

{{story:microbes-friendly}}

Today many people can't see a properly dark sky at all. Streetlights, signs and buildings throw light upwards, and it scatters in the air, washing out the stars. A 2016 study found that about 80 per cent of the world's people live under light-polluted skies, and that the [[@milky-way|Milky Way]] is hidden from more than a third of humanity. In the United States and Europe, the figure for polluted skies is over 99 per cent.

{{story:ladakh}}

Better outdoor lighting can help, such as [[@light-bulb|light bulbs]] shielded so they shine down, not up. Some places are protected on purpose. In 2022, India declared its first dark sky reserve around the [[observatory|observatory]] at Hanle in [[@ladakh|Ladakh]], more than 4,000 m above sea level. Villagers there use shaded lights so the stars stay bright.

One of the best clues is something [[biologist|biologists]] call convergent [[evolution|evolution]]. Animals that aren't closely related often end up with similar bodies because they face similar problems. Sharks, dolphins and extinct ichthyosaurs all evolved the same streamlined shape for swimming fast.

Wings evolved separately in insects, birds, bats and pterosaurs. Eyes have evolved dozens of times, in many different ways. An [[@octopus-smart|octopus]] eye looks a lot like ours, even though our last shared ancestor was a tiny worm-like creature over 500 million years ago.

{{story:octopus-smart}}

The Apollo astronauts got a stronger whiff. Moon dust is fine and sticky, and it clung to their suits as they climbed back into the lunar lander. [[gene|Gene]] Cernan, on Apollo 17 in 1972, said it smelled like spent gunpowder. Others on the crews agreed.

Moon dust is mostly glassy bits of broken rock. It has never been worn smooth by wind or water, so its grains are sharp, like crushed glass. When those fresh edges met the damp air of the cabin, they may have reacted and released a smell. Back on Earth, the samples no longer smell, because they have long since met our air.

{{story:taste-smell-link}}

At full moon and new moon, the Sun, Earth and Moon sit roughly in a line. Their pulls add together, so high tides are higher and low tides are lower. These are called spring tides. You can see how the Moon's shape changes in [[@moon-phases|moon phases]].

At half moon, the Sun and Moon pull at right angles. Their pulls partly cancel out, and the difference between high and low tide is smallest. These gentle tides are called neap tides.

{{story:moon-formed}}

Cold countries use a lot of road salt. In some winters the USA spreads more than 20 million tonnes, according to the US Geological Survey. Cities around the [[@great-lakes|Great Lakes]], like [[@chicago|Chicago]], store it in giant domes and send out fleets of trucks when snow is forecast.

Crews often spray salty water called brine onto roads before a storm arrives. The brine stops ice from bonding firmly to the road, so the snow is easier to clear. Some places have tried unusual extras. Towns in Wisconsin have used salty leftover brine from cheese-making, and others mix in sugar beet juice, which helps salt stick and work at colder temperatures.

{{story:salt}}

Many homes and villages have a kund, or tanka: a round, covered underground tank. The ground around it slopes gently inwards, so rain runs in. The cover keeps the water cool and clean and stops it evaporating.

Villages also dug ponds called [[@india-water-man|johads]] and talabs, and wealthy patrons built stepwells. Chand Baori at Abhaneri, near Jaipur, is more than a thousand years old. Its 3,500 steps zigzag down 13 storeys, about 30 m, to the water. People still visit it today.

{{story:camels}}

Roman traders paid with gold and silver coins, and thousands have been found in South India. The port of Muziris, on the Kerala coast, was one of the busiest. [[archaeologist|Archaeologists]] digging at Pattanam, near [[@kochi|Kochi]], have found Roman pottery and glass that may mark where it stood.

The Roman writer Pliny the Elder complained that India, China and Arabia were draining the empire of its money. Rome bought so much pepper that it built special warehouses for it.

{{story:silk-road}}

Our calendar comes from [[@ancient-rome|ancient Rome]]. According to Roman writers, the very first Roman calendar had just ten months and began in March, when farming and fighting started again after winter. The last month was the tenth, *December*, from the Latin *decem*, "ten". September, October and November meant seven, eight and nine.

The cold weeks of winter were simply left out, without a month. Later, [[@calendar-maths|January and February]] were added to fill the gap. Roman tradition credited the ten-month calendar to Romulus, the legendary founder of the city, and the two new months to the next king, Numa. Eventually the new year moved to January, and the old [[@numbers-names|number names]] ended up two months out of place.

{{story:moon-phases}}

Some of the first coins were made in Lydia (in today's Turkey) around 600 BCE. India's earliest coins, called *punch-marked coins*, were small pieces of silver stamped with symbols such as the sun, animals and trees. A coin made of silver carried value in its metal. Its stamp told people they didn't need to weigh it each time.

Paper money first appeared in China about 1,000 years ago, when merchants grew tired of carrying heavy strings of iron coins. When the Venetian traveller Marco Polo reached China in the 1200s, he was astonished to see people buying goods with printed paper.

{{story:marco-polo}}

A [[@hockey-rules|hockey match]] has four quarters of 15 minutes each. Being a goal down early is not a disaster, because there is plenty of time left. In the 2026 final, Japan scored in the third minute, but India hit back with four goals in the second half.

Once a team equalises, it has [[momentum|momentum]]. Players grow confident, the crowd gets loud, and the other team starts to worry. Penalty corners help too. They are set moves teams practise for hours, often ending in a drag flick, a powerful scooping shot.

{{story:hockey-rules}}

In the early 1980s, jugglers in different countries came up with a way to write tricks as numbers, called siteswap. A "3" means a ball thrown to the height of a normal three-ball cascade. A "5" is a higher throw. Strings like 441 or 531 are recipes for tricks.

Engineers and mathematicians enjoyed the puzzle. Claude Shannon, an American engineer famous for the maths behind digital communication, built juggling machines and wrote a formula linking the number of balls, hands, throw times and empty-hand time. Jugglers still use these [[@number-patterns|number patterns]] to invent new tricks.

{{story:brain-works}}

Koji Kondo, a young composer at Nintendo, wrote the music for *Super Mario Bros.* in 1985. He has explained that he shaped the main theme around the feel of Mario running and jumping, so that the rhythm matched the action. The bouncy tune became one of the most recognised pieces of music in the world.

Kondo also had to think about repetition. A player might hear the same loop dozens of times. The music had to be catchy without being irritating, a balance game composers still worry about.

{{story:orchestra}}

In 1971, two scientists, Joseph Hafele and Richard Keating, took four atomic clocks on ordinary passenger flights around the world, first heading east, then west. When they compared the clocks with ones left behind in Washington, the times differed by billionths of a second, almost exactly as Einstein predicted.

You rely on this effect every time a phone map shows where you are. [[@gps-how|GPS satellites]] carry atomic clocks. They move fast, which slows their time, but they are farther from Earth's gravity, which speeds it up more. Overall, their clocks gain about 38 microseconds a day. Without correction, your position would be wrong by about 10 km by tomorrow.

{{story:gps-how}}

Startups raise money in rounds. Early on, friends, family or "angel investors" put in small amounts. Later come venture capital firms, which invest other people's money in many risky startups, knowing [[@inventions-failed|most will fail]] but hoping one will grow huge.

In return, investors get shares, a [[percentage|percentage]] of the company. If an investor pays ₹1 crore for 10%, the company is valued at ₹10 crore. That number is a bet on the future, not money in the bank. It's simple [[@percentages|percentage]] maths, but it can make a company that has never made a profit look very rich on paper.

{{story:stock-market}}

In 1969, the [[@moon-landing-sites|Apollo 11 lunar module]] carried a small computer with code written by a team at MIT, led by Margaret Hamilton. As the module neared the Moon, alarms went off: the computer was overloaded. Because the software had been designed to drop less important tasks and focus on the most important ones, the landing could continue.

A famous photo shows Hamilton standing beside printouts of the Apollo code, stacked as tall as she was.

{{story:apollo-moon}}

According to the Bible, the Israelites were once united under kings named Saul, David and Solomon. Later the land split in two: Israel in the north, with its capital at Samaria, and Judah in the south, with its capital at [[@jerusalem|Jerusalem]].

How big and powerful David and Solomon's kingdom was is hotly argued. In 1993, [[archaeologist|archaeologists]] found a stone at Tel Dan with an inscription from the 9th century BCE that mentions the "House of David". Many scholars take this as evidence that a king called David founded a ruling family. What his kingdom was like is still debated.

{{story:history-sources}}

Writers were at the heart of the movement. Langston Hughes wrote poems in the [[rhythm|rhythm]] of jazz and the [[@blues|blues]]. Zora Neale Hurston travelled the South collecting folk tales and later wrote the novel *Their Eyes Were Watching God* (1937). Claude McKay, born in Jamaica, wrote fierce poems against injustice.

In 1925 the philosopher Alain Locke published *The New Negro*, a collection of stories, poems and essays. *Negro* was the word commonly used then. The "New Negro" was a person who refused to accept second-class treatment.

{{story:jazz}}

Local people always knew about the lines, but their full size became clear when planes began flying over the region in the 1920s and 1930s. A German mathematician named Maria Reiche spent about 50 years mapping and protecting them, sweeping them clean and measuring them by hand, often from a ladder.

UNESCO named the lines a World Heritage Site in 1994. They're fragile: in 2018, a lorry driver drove across part of the area and left deep tyre tracks.

{{story:ai-what}}

Daily life takes planning. People wear layers of fur and thick felt boots. Cars may be left running or kept in heated garages, because engines can freeze solid. Fish sold at outdoor markets freeze stiff and stand up in buckets like sticks.

Most of the ground in Yakutia is [[permafrost|permafrost]], frozen solid all year. Pipes are often built above ground, and many buildings stand on stilts so their heat doesn't thaw the soil. Schools close for the youngest children only on the most bitterly cold days. Bare skin can get frostbite in minutes, so people cover their faces with scarves that quickly frost white.

{{story:thermometer}}

Ajrakh is a kind of [[@block-printing|block printing]] done with carved wooden blocks and natural dyes, mainly deep indigo blue and madder red. A single cloth can go through up to about 16 stages: soaking, printing a paste that resists dye, dyeing, washing in the river, drying in the sun, and printing again. It can take two or three weeks.

Ajrakh printers once worked in the village of Dhamadka. After the 2001 earthquake and years of water trouble, many families moved and set up a new village. They called it Ajrakhpur, which means "the town of Ajrakh".

{{story:earthquakes}}

Most of the islands are [[atoll|atolls]], rings of coral reef around a lagoon. Corals are tiny animals that build hard skeletons of limestone. Over thousands of years, those skeletons pile up into reefs.

Millions of years ago, there was volcanic rock here. As it slowly sank, corals kept building upwards towards the sunlight. The rock is now deep below, and what's left at the surface is a ring of reef with low islands of broken coral and sand. Nowhere on the islands is more than a few metres above the sea.

{{story:coral-reefs}}

As Buddhism spread, new texts called sutras appeared, many in Sanskrit. They include the Lotus Sutra, the Heart Sutra and the Diamond Sutra, which became central in East Asia. Scholars from China walked to India to collect them. In the 600s CE, the monk Xuanzang studied at [[@nalanda|Nalanda]] and returned home with hundreds of texts, then spent years translating them.

Tibetan Buddhists collected their [[scripture|scriptures]] into two huge sets: the Kangyur, believed to be the Buddha's words, and the Tengyur, made up of commentaries. Together they fill hundreds of volumes.

{{story:printing-press}}

[[@hist-first-newspaper-india|India's first newspaper]] was *Hicky's Bengal Gazette*, started in January 1780 in Calcutta, now [[@kolkata|Kolkata]]. Its owner, James Augustus Hicky, was an Irishman who loved gossip and mocked the officials of the [[@east-india-company|East India Company]], including the Governor-General, Warren Hastings. In 1782 his press was seized and the paper shut down.

Within a century India had hundreds of newspapers in many languages. During the [[@freedom-struggle|freedom struggle]], leaders like Bal Gangadhar Tilak and [[@gandhi|Gandhi]] ran their own papers. Gandhi edited *Young India* and *Harijan*, and wrote much of them himself.

{{story:printing-press}}

Akam poets used a clever code called *tinai*. Five landscapes each matched a mood of love. Mountains meant lovers meeting. Forests and pastures meant patient waiting. Farmland meant quarrels. The seashore meant anxious longing. Dry wasteland meant painful separation.

Each landscape came with its own flowers, birds, animals and gods. The mountain landscape is named after the kurinji flower, which grows in the [[@western-ghats|Western Ghats]] and blooms in great blue waves only every 12 years. So when a poem mentioned kurinji or a peacock, readers knew where they were and how the speaker felt. Poets could pack a lot of feeling into very few words.

{{story:western-ghats}}

In 1799, French soldiers in Egypt found a stone slab carved in 196 BCE. It had the same decree written three ways: in [[@hieroglyphs|hieroglyphs]], in another Egyptian script called Demotic, and in ancient Greek. Scholars could read the Greek. Using it as a key, Jean-François Champollion announced in 1822 that he had cracked [[hieroglyphs|hieroglyphs]]. The Rosetta Stone is now in the British Museum in London.

Translation also carried stories around the world. India's [[@panchatantra-tales|Panchatantra]] was translated into Middle Persian in the 500s CE, then into Arabic as Kalila wa Dimna, then into Hebrew, Latin and many European languages. Along the way the animals' names and some stories changed. Many European fables can be traced back to it.

{{story:ai-what}}

People have sold food from wheels for a long time. In 1866, a Texas rancher called Charles Goodnight fixed a wooden box of shelves and drawers to the back of an old army wagon. It carried beans, flour, coffee and a cook on long cattle drives. Cowboys called it the chuckwagon.

Later came lunch wagons outside factories and [[@ice-cream|ice cream]] vans that played tunes. In India, the same idea lives on in handcarts selling [[@street-food|chaat]], bhutta and kulfi, which are really food trucks without engines.

{{story:startups-entrepreneurs}}

Around the year 1100, Kaifeng and later Hangzhou were among the largest cities on Earth. Writers of the time described restaurants serving dishes from different regions, with waiters who memorised long orders and shouted them to the kitchen. Some places specialised in noodles or vegetarian food, much like restaurants today.

Merchants and officials travelling from distant provinces wanted food from home. Restaurants offering the flavours of Sichuan or the south grew up to serve them, an early version of the regional food courts found in Indian malls.

{{story:ancient-china}}
